PARALLEL PROGRAMMING
====================

THE MODEL USED IN THE EXAMS
-----------------------------
The exams use F#'s Async workflow with Async.Parallel. The pattern is always:

    1. Split the input range into chunks.
    2. Wrap each chunk's computation in async { return ... }.
    3. Collect the async values into a sequence.
    4. Run them all with Async.Parallel |> Async.RunSynchronously.
    5. Combine the partial results (usually Array.fold or Array.maxBy).


CANONICAL TEMPLATE - LEARN THIS SHAPE
---------------------------------------

    let parallelMax (lo : int) (hi : int) (nWorkers : int) : int =
        let chunkSize = max 1 ((hi - lo + 1) / nWorkers)

        Seq.init nWorkers (fun i ->
            let start = lo + i * chunkSize
            let stop  = if i = nWorkers - 1 then hi
                        else start + chunkSize - 1
            async {
                return [start..stop] |> List.maxBy (fun x -> someExpensiveFunction x)
            })
        |> Async.Parallel
        |> Async.RunSynchronously
        |> Array.maxBy (fun x -> someExpensiveFunction x)

The last Array.maxBy is necessary because each worker returns only a local
maximum. You still need a sequential combine step over the nWorkers partial
results to find the global answer.


THE 2022 PARALLEL INIT PATTERN
--------------------------------
The 2022 exam shows a different use case: parallel initialisation of a
mutable 2D array.

    let parInit f rows cols =
        let m = init (fun _ _ -> 0) rows cols
        Array.init (rows * cols) (fun i -> (i / cols, i % cols))
        |> Seq.map (fun (r, c) -> async { set m r c (f r c) })
        |> Async.Parallel
        |> Async.Ignore
        |> Async.RunSynchronously
        m

Each async block writes to a unique cell of a shared mutable array.
This is safe because no two workers write to the same cell.
Async.Ignore discards the unit array result before RunSynchronously.


CHUNKING EDGE CASES
--------------------
The most common exam mistake is not handling the last chunk correctly.
If nWorkers does not divide the range evenly, the last worker must take
the remainder:

    let stop = if i = nWorkers - 1 then hi else start + chunkSize - 1

Without this, the last few elements of the range are silently skipped
and the answer will be wrong.


WHAT THE EXAMS DO NOT TEST
---------------------------
- Cancellation tokens
- MailboxProcessor / agents
- Lock-based synchronisation
- Task-based parallelism (System.Threading.Tasks)

Stick to async { }, Async.Parallel, and Async.RunSynchronously.
That is the entire parallel API the exams use.


COMMON MISTAKES
---------------
- Calling Async.RunSynchronously on each individual async instead of on the
  combined Async.Parallel result. This runs everything sequentially and
  defeats the purpose entirely.
- Forgetting the final combine step. Each worker returns a local answer,
  not the global one.
- Off-by-one in chunk boundaries, especially for the last worker.
  Always use "if i = nWorkers - 1 then hi" for the final stop value.
- Using Array.Parallel.map from System.Array instead of Async.Parallel.
  The exams explicitly use the async workflow style.
